# ~/.bashrc — tracked in the dotfiles repo as omarchy/bashrc, symlinked here.
#
# WHY THIS MACHINE RUNS BASH WHILE THE OTHER TWO RUN ZSH
#
# Omarchy ships no zsh support at all: /usr/share/omarchy/default/ has an
# alacritty/, a ghostty/, a hypr/ and a bash/ — and nothing for zsh. That bash/
# directory is not a courtesy either, it is 780 lines and a real part of the
# distro: ~45 aliases and functions (`n`, `cx`, `t`, `hdl`, `ff`, `zd`, `rsw`,
# `iso2sd`, the worktree and ssh-reconnect helpers), tab-completion for the
# `omarchy` dispatcher, EDITOR/BROWSER wired to omarchy-launch-*, mise
# activation, and the `try` scratch-workspace hook.
#
# None of it ports cleanly. The completion is 129 lines of COMPREPLY/compgen
# with no zsh equivalent, and two files index arrays from 0 — which zsh, indexing
# from 1, would run without complaint and get wrong. Porting it would also mean
# re-porting after every `omarchy update`.
#
# So this machine stays on bash and the repo's .zshrc keeps serving the Mac and
# the WSL box. The trade is deliberate: uniformity loses to the distro here,
# because the distro is the thing that keeps getting updates.
#
# The first three lines below are Omarchy's stock ~/.bashrc, from /etc/skel,
# unchanged. Everything personal goes underneath — which is where the stock
# file's own comment invites it.

# Omarchy environment (OMARCHY_PATH + PATH), needed even for non-interactive shells
[[ -r /usr/share/omarchy/default/bash/env-bootstrap ]] && source /usr/share/omarchy/default/bash/env-bootstrap

# If not running interactively, don't do anything else (leave this above the rc source)
[[ $- != *i* ]] && return

# All the default Omarchy aliases and functions
# (don't mess with these directly, just overwrite them here!)
source "$OMARCHY_PATH/default/bash/rc"

# ============================================================================
#  Personal layer
# ============================================================================
# Everything below is additive. Nothing here redefines an Omarchy alias — check
# with `alias | grep '^alias X='` before adding one, and prefer a new name to
# shadowing a default, or `omarchy update` improvements stop reaching you.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Secrets
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API keys, git-ignored, chmod 600. Same template as the other two machines
# (.zshrc.secrets.example) — it is only `export` lines, so it is shell-agnostic;
# the file is named for the shell that sources it.
[[ -f "$HOME/.bashrc.secrets" ]] && source "$HOME/.bashrc.secrets"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Aliases — additions only
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Omarchy already defines ls, lsa, lt and lta over eza. It has no long-listing
# `ll` or plain `la`, which is muscle memory from the other two machines.
if command -v eza &>/dev/null; then
  alias ll="eza -lah --group-directories-first --icons"
  alias la="eza -a --group-directories-first --icons"
fi

# Omarchy's `n` opens nvim (and `n` with no argument opens the cwd). These are
# the muscle-memory spellings from the WSL box and the Mac.
alias vim="nvim"
alias vi="nvim"
alias v="nvim"

# Omarchy exports BAT_THEME and routes man pages through bat, but leaves `cat`
# alone. --paging=never keeps it a drop-in replacement inside pipes.
command -v bat &>/dev/null && alias cat="bat --paging=never"

# Omarchy has `d` for docker; compose gets its own two.
alias dcu="docker compose up"
alias dcd="docker compose down"

# Parity with zshconfig/zshreload on the other machines.
alias bashconfig="nvim ~/.bashrc"
alias bashreload="source ~/.bashrc"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  atuin — shell history in SQLite (Ctrl-R)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one tool from the shared toolbox that Omarchy does not ship. It needs
# bash-preexec, because bash has no native preexec/precmd hooks the way zsh does
# — that shim is what lets atuin see a command before it runs. It MUST be
# sourced before `atuin init`, and last in this file, since bash-preexec wraps
# PROMPT_COMMAND and starship (loaded by Omarchy's rc above) sets it.
if command -v atuin &>/dev/null; then
  [[ -r /usr/share/bash-preexec/bash-preexec.sh ]] &&
    source /usr/share/bash-preexec/bash-preexec.sh
  eval "$(atuin init bash)"
fi
